  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Upstream ridging over the Plains this \nafternoon is continuing to make slow eastward progress toward the \nregion...becoming situated overhead during the day Monday. At the \nsurface, ~1030mb high pressure currently sits over the western Great \nLakes gradually sagging south into the Ohio Valley on Monday. Little \nchange to this through midweek as ridging and high pressure aid to \ndominate northern MI's sensible weather. By Thursday, attention \nflips to an approaching shortwave trough and attendant surface \nreflection that are expected to trek by to our northwest. This \nlikely to drag a frontal boundary across northern MI, perhaps \nreintroducing rain shower chances back into parts of the area. \nBeyond this, some potential for a larger system to build across the \nnation's midsection next weekend.\n\nForecast Details: Lingering lake induced cloud cover across parts of \nnorthwest and northeast lower early this afternoon is expected to \ncontinue to slowly erode through the remainder of the afternoon and \nevening hours setting the stage for partly to mainly clear skies \ntonight. Fairly high chances for winds to decouple overnight given a \nlackadaisical pressure gradient thanks to incoming high pressure, \nperhaps leading to the development of patchy fog tonight. Warmer \nsouthwesterly flow to prevail Monday aiding to draw in a much warmer \nairmass for much of the week ahead. High temperatures progged in the \n50s for much of the week ahead, perhaps some lower 60s in favored \ndownsloping areas by Tuesday - Thursday. Mid-November normal highs \nrange from 39-45 degrees. \n\nLatest trends support generally low end precip chances on Thursday \nas a wave ejects out of the Rockies and slides northeast across the \nnorthern Plains and upper MS Valley into Ontario. This should drag a \nfrontal boundary across northern MI with at least low-end isolated \nto scattered shower chances.\n\nLooking into the weekend, uncertainty really increases with respect \nto the details, but latest longer range guidance trends support a \nmore robust wave ejecting into the Plains by Saturday. Associated \nrapidly deepening sfc low pressure to follow suit on a similar path \nto Thursday's -- from the Plains to MN/upper MS Valley into Ontario. \nWhile this system will likely have occluded by the time it reaches \nour vicinity, a return of rain chances are probable, potentially \nfollowed by a cool down late in the weekend with at least some \npotential for a return of some lake effect snow shower activity.",
